WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.470
Picture a rope made of
three twisted strands:

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.760
one white, one red, one dark.

3
00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.700
The Bhagavad Gita teaches that all of
nature is woven from three such strands,

4
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.150
called gunas.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.350
Many Hindus also prize
three virtues: non-violence,

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.960
detachment and self-restraint.

7
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.230
Yet the same text warns that
these strands bind the spirit.

8
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.330
Even the strand of goodness.

9
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.070
So why would goodness tie you down?

10
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.620
In this mission, you'll meet each virtue,

11
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:36.110
and see how the Gita sorts food
and action by the three strands.

12
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.230
Then you'll weigh up whether living
virtuously is enough to achieve liberation.

13
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.370
Your mission: find out why the Gita says
that even goodness can tie you down.
